# servicing_vehicle.k26asm - a ten-tonne class servicer that spends
# what it thrusts.
#
# WHAT THIS IS. A modelled article. Nothing in this file is cited and
# nothing here is a claim about any vehicle: the dry mass, the tank,
# the hull dimensions, the thruster layout and the specific impulse
# are all chosen, and each is disclosed as chosen on its own
# provenance line. What the article is for is a control task in which
# propellant runs out, which needs a craft whose mass falls as it
# manoeuvres.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT THE CREW VEHICLE BESIDE IT. `crew_vehicle_10t.k26asm`
# carries mass and inertia figures taken from a published standard,
# and its hull geometry was solved so that this compiler's own closed
# forms return exactly those figures. Splitting its ten tonnes into a
# dry hull and a tank would move the derived tensor off the figures it
# reproduces, because a tank has its own placement and its own
# moments, and the standard publishes no propellant mass to split off
# in any case. So the tanked article is a separate asset and the cited
# one is left alone.
#
# WHAT IS KEPT FROM IT, AND WHY. The size class, the actuator count
# and the actuator authority. The hull is a capsule 1.93 m in radius
# with its segment endpoints 1.79 m either side of the centre, which
# is within a quarter of a per cent of the other article's dimensions,
# and the dry mass and the tank together make ten tonnes. What this
# compiler then derives was measured back out of a compiled world, by
# commanding a known wheel torque and reading the angular acceleration
# it produced: 17020, 42031 and 42039 kg m^2 about the three body
# axes, against 17000, 42000 and 42000 for the other article. Every
# one is within 0.1 per cent, so the control problem is the one
# already characterised: the same twelve body-fixed thrusters at the
# same 400 N, the same three reaction wheels at the same 5 N m, the
# same total mass, and therefore the same accelerations, the same
# rates and the same integration margin. The dimensions themselves are
# chosen rather than solved for anything, and the agreement is what
# the choice was checked against rather than what it was derived from.
#
# WHAT THE TANK HOLDS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. Thirty kilogrammes on a
# ten tonne vehicle, which is three parts in a thousand. That is not a
# spacecraft's mission load and is not offered as one. It is the
# allocation for one close manoeuvre, and the article is written that
# way on purpose: proximity operations are cheap in velocity change
# and expensive in waste, so a load sized for a whole mission would
# never bind on a single approach and would leave the model
# unexercised. What binds here is one manoeuvre's share.
#
# The tank sits 1.2 m aft of the hull's centre, so the vehicle's
# centre of mass travels as it empties: at a full tank it is
# 1.2 * m / (dry + m) aft of the hull's centre, which is 3.6 mm, and
# at an empty one it is at the hull's centre. Thruster torques are
# taken about the centre of mass, so they travel with it, by about two
# parts in a thousand against moment arms near two metres. The inertia
# travels with it too, and by how much is checkable: the tank's own
# share of the transverse moment about the body origin is
# 30 * 1.2^2 + (1/3) * 30 * (0.16^2 + 0.16^2), which is 43.71 kg m^2,
# and measured across a burn to depletion the transverse moment falls
# by 0.104 per cent of 42031, which is 43.6. The effect is real and
# correctly signed and it is small at this fill, because a propellant
# load of three parts in a thousand cannot move a centre of mass far.
# The offset is here because a centred tank would have hidden the
# effect entirely rather than showing it small.
#
# The tank is a box of half extent 0.16 m, which is 0.0328 cubic
# metres and puts thirty kilogrammes at about 915 kg per cubic metre,
# the order of a storable propellant. Its placement and its shape are
# an arrangement rather than a measurement: tank geometry is not
# published for the vehicles this article stands in for, so this
# component carries the modelled status and the compiler reports it.
#
# THE SPECIFIC IMPULSE. 300 s on every thruster, which is the right
# order for a storable bipropellant thruster and is not read from a
# source. Every thruster carries the same figure because this article
# has one kind of thruster; a craft whose main engine and attitude
# thrusters differ is what the calibration article next to this one is
# for, and giving them a spread here would make the published delta-v
# channel depend on which thruster the policy last had propellant for
# rather than on the budget it has.
#
# At 300 s a thruster at full throttle spends 400 / (300 * 9.80665),
# which is 0.13598 kg/s, and twelve of them at once spend 1.632 kg/s.
# A full tank therefore lasts 18.4 s if a policy opens everything, and
# the whole load is worth 300 * 9.80665 * ln(10000 / 9970), which is
# 8.84 m/s of velocity change.

assembly servicing_vehicle
    frame x_to_port

    provenance mass "chosen: a 9970 kg dry hull and a 30 kg manoeuvre allocation, making a ten tonne class servicer; no article is claimed and no figure is cited" modelled
    provenance hull_geometry "chosen: a capsule of 1.93 m radius and 1.79 m half length, placing this article in the same size class as the crew vehicle asset beside it" modelled
    provenance inertia "computed: this compiler's closed forms over the declared components at the declared fill" computed
    provenance tank "a chosen tank placement, held aft of the hull's centre so the centre of mass travels as it empties; this is an arrangement and not a measurement" modelled
    provenance isp "a chosen figure of the right order for a storable bipropellant thruster; no source is cited because no article is claimed" modelled
    provenance thrust "chosen to match the actuator authority of the crew vehicle asset beside it, so that the control problem is the one already characterised" modelled
    provenance thruster_layout "two rings at the cylinder ends in opposed pairs, chosen and not derived; no thruster position is published for any vehicle this article stands in for" modelled
    provenance wheel_limits "computed: the torque that nulls 0.2 deg/s about the transverse axis in a minute at this article's transverse inertia, that torque held for 100 s, and 1000 rad/s when saturated" computed
    provenance wheel_friction "a chosen friction model of the right order for a wheel of this size; no figure for the viscous, Coulomb or dead-rate terms is published or derived here" modelled

    component hull
        mass 9970.0
        at 0.0 0.0 0.0
        collider capsule -1.79 0.0 0.0 1.79 0.0 0.0 1.93
    end

    component tank
        mass 30.0
        at -1.2 0.0 0.0
        collider box 0.16 0.16 0.16
        propellant
    end

    # Two thrusters push the vehicle toward its forward end and two
    # push it away. Each pair sits on opposite sides of the hull at
    # one ring, so firing a pair together is a force through the
    # centre of mass and firing one alone is a pitch or yaw command.
    thruster close_yp
        at -1.79 1.93 0.0
        dir 1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster close_ym
        at -1.79 -1.93 0.0
        dir 1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster brake_yp
        at 1.79 1.93 0.0
        dir -1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster brake_ym
        at 1.79 -1.93 0.0
        dir -1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end

    # Eight lateral thrusters, four to a plane. Each group of two is
    # one forward and one aft of the centre of mass with the same
    # direction, so the pair translates and either one alone turns.
    thruster lat_yp_fwd
        at 1.79 -1.93 0.0
        dir 0.0 1.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_yp_aft
        at -1.79 -1.93 0.0
        dir 0.0 1.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_ym_fwd
        at 1.79 1.93 0.0
        dir 0.0 -1.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_ym_aft
        at -1.79 1.93 0.0
        dir 0.0 -1.0 0.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zp_fwd
        at 1.79 0.0 -1.93
        dir 0.0 0.0 1.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zp_aft
        at -1.79 0.0 -1.93
        dir 0.0 0.0 1.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zm_fwd
        at 1.79 0.0 1.93
        dir 0.0 0.0 -1.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zm_aft
        at -1.79 0.0 1.93
        dir 0.0 0.0 -1.0
        thrust 400.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end

    # Three reaction wheels, which spend nothing. A policy that turns
    # the vehicle on its wheels rather than on opposed thrusters keeps
    # its propellant for the approach, and that is one of the things
    # this article is here to let a controller discover.
    wheel wheel_x
        axis 1.0 0.0 0.0
        spin_inertia 0.5
        max_momentum 500.0
        max_torque 5.0
        viscous 1.0e-5
        coulomb 2.0e-4
        dead_rate 1.0e-3
    end
    wheel wheel_y
        axis 0.0 1.0 0.0
        spin_inertia 0.5
        max_momentum 500.0
        max_torque 5.0
        viscous 1.0e-5
        coulomb 2.0e-4
        dead_rate 1.0e-3
    end
    wheel wheel_z
        axis 0.0 0.0 1.0
        spin_inertia 0.5
        max_momentum 500.0
        max_torque 5.0
        viscous 1.0e-5
        coulomb 2.0e-4
        dead_rate 1.0e-3
    end
end
